== P5: Schwinger / thermal-threshold principle (probe wall in dS, needs hbar) ==
PASS P5a probe wall in dS(H = 1/L): the instanton is an S^3 of radius R = L sin(theta0) with tan(theta0) = H/a  (a = DeltaP/(3 sigma)), i.e. 1/R^2 = H^2 + a^2 (matches the embedding result of the audit)
PASS P5b exact probe-dS bounce action B = 2 pi^2 L^3 sigma f(a/H), f(phi) = (1 + 2 phi^2)/sqrt(1 + phi^2) - 2 phi; flat limit f -> 1/(4 phi^3) reproduces B = pi^2 sigma/(2 a^3)
PASS P5c B/S_dS = (2 pi sigma/H) f(a/H): in units of the de Sitter entropy the action is the wall's self-gravity acceleration 2 pi sigma over H times a function of a/H
   sigma_th = b0*hbar*sqrt(phi**2 + 1)/(2*pi**2*L**3*(2*phi**2 - 2*phi*sqrt(phi**2 + 1) + 1))   (so sigma_th/H ~ hbar H^2/(2 pi^2 f) ~ (l_Planck H)^2 ~ 1e-120: the wall is an extreme probe)
   at b0 = 1 and hbar H^2 = 1e-122: sigma_th/H = 7.2505e-124, x = e/E = 1.5739e-123, relative shift of a/H = 7.8697e-124
PASS P5d the threshold fixes sigma (through hbar), not k: at the threshold x = e/E ~ 1e-123, so a/H = k(1 - x/2)/(2 sqrt(3 pi)) is unchanged to a relative 1e-123: (iv) leaves k, hence a0/H, exactly as free as before
   at the target a/H = 1/Z: f = 0.69872, so B = 0.6987 * 2 pi^2 L^3 sigma

== P6: thresholds of the wall's own geometry (probe limit) ==
   a = H crossover (Schwinger radius = horizon radius):  k = 2*sqrt(3)*sqrt(pi) = 6.1400 ; the puzzle target sits at a = H/Z, i.e. Z = 5.7888 times below the crossover
PASS P6a the a = H crossover needs k = 2 sqrt(3 pi) = 6.13: it is a definition of the crossover, ratio to the target k* = 3/(2 sqrt 2) is exactly Z (no information)
PASS P6b wall at the horizon: a(R) = sqrt(1/R^2 - H^2) -> infinity as R -> 0; equatorial wall R = 1/H has a = 0; a = H/Z sits at R = (1/H)/sqrt(1 + 1/Z^2)... neither limit is a finite a = H/Z

== pairs of declared principles: which give a point? ==
PASS PAIRS P1&P2 -> DeltaP = 0 = 6 pi sigma^2 forces sigma = 0 (no wall); P1'&P3 -> A = 0 needs DeltaP < 0 but x = 1 has DeltaP = 1/2 > 0 (empty); P2&P3 contradictory: the only declared PAIR that gives a point is P1&P3 (x = 1, s = 1/sqrt(12 pi)); P1&P4 gives the one-parameter family x = 1/n, s = sqrt(x(1 - x/2)/(6 pi))

